Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||07-30-2001||09:30 PM||||You don't 'unplug the cat'.<BR>Simply remove it. If you don't know it's location crawl under the car, look up at the starter and it's the large area of the exhaust system next to the passenger compartment. If you live in a state that does emissions testing, you will need to replace it.<BR>If only one and not both are glowing, you are running lean on that side. Try some injector cleaner in it, you may also have a bad o2 sensor or other problems. Like your in need of a tune-up (plugs and wires full ignition check).||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fiero_24||07-30-2001||09:37 PM||||Check the Evaporative fuel canister.  If it goes out it sucks fuel instead of air out of the gas tank.  This has happened to me on all 3 Fiero's I have owned.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||LivoniaFieroGT||07-30-2001||09:49 PM||||Had similar problem and the culprit was a small crack in the EGR tube.  The exhaust manifold was turning cherry red and I couldn't get the idle down either.||||reg||1
Z||000009||bHooper||07-30-2001||11:48 PM||||timing and exhaust leaks can also be the cause of this.  have you done any work on your car?<P>if not, then the plugged cat is probably the most logical theory.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000010||84 2in1||07-31-2001||02:35 AM||||My computer was shot. my manifold would glow bright red and the exhaust would nearly burn you. changed the comp and o2 sensor and it never ran better.-B-||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||07-31-2001||02:46 PM||||cracked EGR tube is the most common cause. Also any vacume leaks.||||reg||1
